TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "Pick and Roll (PNR) offensive strategy",
    "PNR failure definition",
    "Pull-up jump shots",
    "Offensive counter strategies",
    "Offensive efficiency metrics (points per possession, lift)",
    "Play-type sequencing"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to define 'PNR fail' (e.g., low PPP, turnover, specific shot outcome)?",
    "How to identify 'PULL_UP' shots from play-by-play data or derived play types?",
    "How to establish a causal or conditional link between PNR failure and subsequent PULL_UP shots as a 'counter'?",
    "How to measure 'lift' in offensive efficiency for the counter, and what is the appropriate baseline?",
    "How to aggregate and analyze data across 'units' (teams, lineups) to assess 'league-wide' patterns and meet the >=70% and median lift criteria?",
    "How to account for confounding factors (e.g., player skill, defensive schemes, game situation, score effects)?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No historical evidence was available, so it establishes nothing."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No historical evidence was available, so it leaves all questions open."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The available data (play-by-play, derived play-type possession records) appears sufficient to identify PNR possessions, their outcomes, and subsequent pull-up shots.",
    "The definition of 'PNR fail' is critical and will influence the findings.",
    "The choice of baseline for 'lift' is crucial for a valid assessment of the counter strategy's effectiveness."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The precise definitions used in the 'derived play-type possession records' are not fully specified (e.g., how PNR is identified, how PPP is calculated, how 'PULL_UP' is classified).",
    "The frequency of the specific sequence 'PNR fail followed by PULL_UP' for individual units (teams/lineups) is unknown, which impacts power."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was retrieved, so there was no historical context to change the decision."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.7,
    "blocking_constraints": []
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior work was retrieved that directly addresses this specific research question."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "The choice to take a pull-up shot after a PNR failure could be influenced by numerous factors beyond a direct counter-strategy, such as shot clock pressure, defensive scheme adjustments, specific player tendencies, or pre-designed secondary options. These factors could confound the observed 'lift'."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "The specific sequence 'PNR fail followed by PULL_UP' may occur infrequently for some 'units' (teams or lineups), potentially leading to small sample sizes when trying to meet the '>=70% of units' and 'median lift > 2pp' criteria. This could reduce statistical power for robust conclusions across units."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Defining 'PNR fail' (e.g., specific PPP threshold, turnover, missed shot) and accurately identifying 'PULL_UP' shots from play-by-play data, or relying on derived play-type records without full visibility into their definitions, introduces potential measurement error and subjectivity."
    },
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "'PNR fail' is a label derived from the outcome of a possession, and 'lift' is a derived metric. The choice of thresholds and baselines for these derived labels can significantly impact the findings and may not perfectly capture the strategic intent."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.4,
    "rejected": 0.3,
    "untestable": 0.1,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.1
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The average 'lift' in Points Per Possession (PPP) when a PULL_UP shot occurs immediately following a 'PNR fail' possession, compared to a defined baseline, aggregated across NBA teams (units) from the 2022-23 to 2025-26 seasons.",
    "unit": "The primary unit of analysis will be a single NBA team within a single season. The 'lift' will be calculated for each team, and then aggregated to assess the league-wide criteria.",
    "sample": "All NBA regular season and playoff games from 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 (complete as of data availability date). A 'PNR fail' possession is defined as any PNR possession (from derived play-type data) that results in 0 points and is not an offensive rebound leading to a new possession. A 'PULL_UP' is identified from play-by-play action types/subtypes or derived play-type records. We will consider sequences where a PULL_UP shot attempt occurs on the immediate next possession (same offensive team) after a 'PNR fail' possession.",
    "power": "To ensure sufficient power, a minimum threshold of 50 instances of 'PNR fail followed by PULL_UP' will be required for a team-season unit to be included in the '>=70% of units' calculation. If a unit does not meet this threshold, it will be excluded. The overall sample size across all included units is expected to be large enough for robust league-wide statistics.",
    "controls": "The 'lift' will be calculated as: (PPP of PULL_UPs following PNR fail) - (Baseline PPP of PULL_UPs). The baseline PPP for PULL_UPs will be the team's season-average PPP on all PULL_UP attempts, regardless of preceding play type. This controls for team-specific pull-up efficiency. We will also perform sensitivity analysis using alternative baselines (e.g., league-average PULL_UP PPP, or team's PPP on all possessions immediately following a PNR fail, regardless of shot type). Game context (score differential, shot clock, period) will be explored as potential confounders in secondary analysis if initial results are ambiguous.",
    "falsification": "The primary falsification test is whether the observed 'lift' for PULL_UPs following PNR fail is statistically significantly greater than 0 for a sufficient number of units, and whether the median lift across units exceeds 2pp. A secondary falsification could involve comparing the 'lift' of PULL_UPs to other shot types following a PNR fail, to ensure it's a specific counter, not a general regression to the mean for any subsequent shot.",
    "multiplicity": "The primary hypothesis involves two conditions: lift>0 in >=70% of units AND median lift>2pp. These will be tested jointly. No formal correction for multiple comparisons will be applied for the individual unit tests (lift>0 for each unit) as they contribute to a single aggregate metric (percentage of units).",
    "decision_rule": "The research hypothesis is supported if: 1) At least 70% of team-season units (meeting minimum sample size) show a statistically significant 'lift' > 0 (p < 0.05) for PULL_UPs following PNR fail, AND 2) The median 'lift' across all such units is greater than 2.0 points per possession. Otherwise, the hypothesis is rejected or deemed inconclusive if power is insufficient.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 (complete as of 2026-06-21) regular season and playoffs play-by-play and derived play-type possession records."
  }
}

